:title("Parser state memoization test") ;
:description("Guards the purity gate of the parser's -lf (found list) and -lb (block list):
a production whose reachable subtree contains a :script() carries parser state, so its
result is NOT a function of (rule, position, whitespace rule, skipping) alone and must not
be memoized or block-listed.

The grammar makes the two ways a :script() can hide from a naive subtree walk observable.
First and Second share the prefix Probe, and First always fails on the trailing '!', so the
parser applies Probe at position 0 exactly TWICE. Each application runs both scripts once:

  * NoteI is reached only through a chain of Identifiers (Chain -> Marked -> NoteI), so a
    walk that does not follow the Identifier links into the a-grammar would call Probe pure.
  * NoteW is reached only through the CodeChilds of the :whitespace() command of Spaced, so
    a walk that only looks at Childs would call Spaced pure.

Both scripts count their runs on the persistent parser stack and Check asserts the counts,
so a wrongly memoized (or wrongly blocked) production shows up as a count of 1 and the parse
fails. Run with no flag, with -lf and with -lb: all three must print the same line and
exit 0.") ;


:startRule(Test) ;

// First always fails at the '!', which is what makes the parser apply Probe at
// position 0 a second time - the only way a found-list hit can ever be observed.
Test    = First | Second ;
First   = Probe "!" ;
Second  = Probe Check ;
Probe   = Chain Spaced ;

// Path 1: the :script() is reachable only through Identifier links.
Chain   = Marked ;
Marked  = "a" NoteI ;

// Path 2: the :script() is reachable only through a :whitespace() argument.
Spaced  = :whitespace(WsNote) "b" ;
WsNote  = NoteI2 ;

// The counters live on the stack that survives between the :script() calls of one
// parse run - exactly the parser state that a memoized result would skip.
NoteI = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = pop()
        if (s == null) s = {i: 0, w: 0}
        s.i++
        push(s)
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

NoteI2 = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = pop()
        if (s == null) s = {i: 0, w: 0}
        s.w++
        push(s)
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// Both scripts must have run once per Probe application, so twice. Returning a
// token that cannot match is how a :script() rule reports a failure.
Check = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = pop()
        if (s == null) s = {i: 0, w: 0}
        push(s)
        if (s.i === 2 && s.w === 2) {
            println("parser state memo test passed")
            return undefined
        }
        println("FAIL: the scripts ran i=" + s.i + " w=" + s.w + " time(s), want i=2 w=2")
        return abnf.newToken("\x01stateful-production-was-memoized", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
